  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/09798cb8c62aabe483490ad24f4c13918138c4292ffb19a8d6612ab4efe18497.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{5}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units up to give $y=-{5}^{x}+4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
